e Carnevale-Voll Conjecture (https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL23/Habsieger/habs3.html)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; Crawled: 6.4 years ago; ## Explicit Asymptotics for Signed Binomial Sums and Applications to the Carnevale-Voll Conjecture ... Carnevale and Voll conjectured that Image: $\\sum_j (-1)^j{\\lambda_1\\choose j}{\\lambda_2\\choose j}\\neq0$ when Image: $\\lambda_1$ and Image: $\\lambda_2$ are two distinct integers.\n\n## Explicit Asymptotics for Signed Binomial Sums and Applications to the Carnevale-Voll Conjecture\n\n### Laurent Habsieger\nUniversité de Lyon, CNRS UMR 5208\nUniversité Claude Bernard Lyon 1\nInstitut Camille Jordan\n43 boulevard du 11 novembre 1918\n69622 Villeurbanne Cedex\nFrance\n\nAbstract:\n\nCarnevale and Voll conjectured that Image: $\\sum_j (-1)^j{\\lambda_1\\choose j}{\\lambda_2\\choose j}\\neq0$ when Image: $\\lambda_1$ and Image: $\\lambda_2$ are two distinct integers. We check the conjecture when either Image: $\\lambda_2$ or Image: $\\lambda_1-\\lambda_2$ is small. We investigate the asymptotic behaviour of the sum when the ratio Image: $r:=\\lambda_1/\\lambda_2$ is fixed and Image: $\\lambda_2$ goes to infinity. We find an explicit range Image: $r\\ge 5.8362$ on which the conjecture is true. We show that the conjecture is almost surely true for any fixed r. For r close to 1, we give several explicit intervals on which the conjecture is also true.\n\nFull version:  pdf,    dvi,    ps,    latex,     Maple code    \n\nReceived July 3 2019; revised versions received February 5 2020; February 9 2020; May 27 2020. Published in Journal of Integer Sequences, June 10 2020.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities (https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.10203)\nciteturn2academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.1 years ago; We prove that the dimension of the feasible region of densities of permutation patterns of size at most k is equal to the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k.\nTitle: The dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities\nAuthors: Frederik Garbe, Daniel Kral, Alexandru Malekshahian, Raul Penaguiao\nDate: Mon Sep 18 23:06:47 2023\n\nA classical result of Erd\\H{o}s, Lov\\'asz and Spencer from the late 1970s asserts that the dimension of the feasible region of densities of graphs with at most k vertices in large graphs is equal to the number of non-trivial connected graphs with at most k vertices. Indecomposable permutations play the role of connected graphs in the realm of permutations, and Glebov et al. showed that pattern densities of indecomposable permutations are independent, i.e., the dimension of the feasible region of densities of permutation patterns of size at most k is at least the number of non-trivial indecomposable permutations of size at most k. However, this lower bound is not tight already for k=3. We prove that the dimension of the feasible region of densities of permutation patterns of size at most k is equal to the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k. The proof exploits an interplay between algebra and combinatorics inherent to the study of Lyndon words.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1\n3\n47\n6\n23\n11\nArticle 20.6.7\nJournal of Integer S (https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL23/Habsieger/habs3.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; Explicit Asymptotics for Signed Binomial ... investigate the asymptotic behaviour of the sum when the ratio r := λ1/λ2 is ﬁxed and\n1\n3\n47\n6\n23\n11\nArticle 20.6.7\nJournal of Integer Sequences, Vol. 23 (2020),\n2\nExplicit Asymptotics for Signed Binomial\nSums and Applications to the Carnevale-Voll\nConjecture\nLaurent Habsieger\nUniversit´e de Lyon, CNRS UMR 5208\nUniversit´e Claude Bernard Lyon 1\nInstitut Camille Jordan\n43 boulevard du 11 novembre 1918\n69622 Villeurbanne Cedex\nFrance\nhabsieger@math.univ-lyon1.fr\nAbstract\nCarnevale and Voll conjectured that P\nj(−1)j\u0000λ1\nj\n\u0001\u0000λ2\nj\n\u0001\n̸= 0 when λ1 and λ2 are two\ndistinct integers. We check the conjecture when either λ2 or λ1 −λ2 is small. We\ninvestigate the asymptotic behaviour of the sum when the ratio r := λ1/λ2 is ﬁxed and\nλ2 goes to inﬁnity. We ﬁnd an explicit range r ≥5.8362 on which the conjecture is\ntrue. We show that the conjecture is almost surely true for any ﬁxed r. For r close to\n1, we give several explicit intervals on which the conjecture is also true.\n1\nIntroduction\nCarnevale and Voll [1] studied Dirichlet series enumerating orbits of Cartesian products of\nmaps whose orbits distributions are modelled on the distributions of ﬁnite index subgroups\nof free abelian groups of ﬁnite ranks. For Cartesian products of more than three maps they\nestablish a natural boundary for meromorphic continuation.\nFor products of two maps,\nthey formulate two combinatorial conjectures that prove the existence of such a natural\nboundary. These conjectures state that some explicit polynomials have no unitary factors,\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProceedings of the 12th European Conference on Combinatorics, Graph Theory and Applications (https://journals.muni.cz/eurocomb/article/download/35599/31476/58989)\nciteturn2search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.3 years ago; The dimension of the feasible region of ... the dimension of the feasible region of homomorphic densities of graphs with at most ... identify a larger set of permutations, which are called Lyndon permutations, whose\nProceedings of the 12th European Conference on Combinatorics, Graph Theory and Applications\nEUROCOMB’23\nPrague, August 28 - September 1, 2023\nThe dimension of the feasible region of\npattern densities\n(Extended abstract)\nFrederik Garbe∗\nDaniel Král’∗\nAlexandru Malekshahian†\nRaul Penaguiao‡\nAbstract\nA classical result of Erdős, Lovász and Spencer from the late 1970s asserts that\nthe dimension of the feasible region of homomorphic densities of graphs with at most\nk vertices in large graphs is equal to the number of connected graphs with at most k\nvertices. Glebov et al. showed that pattern densities of indecomposable permutations\nare independent, i.e., the dimension of the feasible region of densities of k-patterns is\nat least the number of non-trivial indecomposable permutations of size at most k. We\nidentify a larger set of permutations, which are called Lyndon permutations, whose\npattern densities are independent, and show that the dimension of the feasible region\nof densities of k-patterns is equal to the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations\nof size at most k.\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.5817/CZ.MUNI.EUROCOMB23-065\n1\nIntroduction\nA classical result of Erdős, Lovász and Spencer [8] describes the independence of homomor-\nphic densities of graphs in large graphs. Informally speaking, they showed that homomor-\nphic densities of connected graphs are independent and actually determine the densities of\n∗Faculty of Informatics, Masaryk University, Botanická 68A, 602 00 Brno, Czech Republic.\nE-\nmail:\n{garbe,dkral}@fi.muni.cz.\nSupported by the MUNI Award in Science and Humanities\n(MUNI/I/1677/2018) of the Grant Agency of Masaryk University.\n†Department of Mathematics, King’s College London. E-mail: alexandru.malekshahian@kcl.ac.uk.\n‡Max Planck Institute for the Sciences,\nInselstraße 22,\n04103 Leipzig,\nGermany.\nE-mail:\nraul.penaguiao@mis.mpg.de.\n471--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExplicit Asymptotics for Signed Binomial Sums and Applications to Carnevale-Voll Conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.09704)\nciteturn2academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; Title: Explicit Asymptotics for Signed Binomial Sums and Applications to Carnevale-Voll Conjecture ... We investigate the asymptotic behaviour of their sum when the ratio r := $\\lambda$ 1 /$\\lambda$ 2 is fixed and $\\lambda$ 2 goes to infinity.\nTitle: Explicit Asymptotics for Signed Binomial Sums and Applications to Carnevale-Voll Conjecture\nAuthors: Laurent Habsieger\nDate: Wed Jun 17 08:03:25 2020\n\nCarnevale and Voll conjectured that j (--1) j $\\lambda$ 1 j $\\lambda$ 2 j = 0 when $\\lambda$ 1 and $\\lambda$ 2 are two distinct integers. We check the conjecture when either $\\lambda$ 2 or $\\lambda$ 1 -- $\\lambda$ 2 is small. We investigate the asymptotic behaviour of their sum when the ratio r := $\\lambda$ 1 /$\\lambda$ 2 is fixed and $\\lambda$ 2 goes to infinity. We find an explicit range r $\\ge$ 5.8362 on which the conjecture is true. We show that the conjecture is almost surely true for any fixed r. For r close to 1, we give several explicit intervals on which the conjecture is also true.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities∗ (https://raulpenaguiao.github.io/docs/Mathdocs/2024feasibleregiondimension.pdf)\nciteturn2search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Borga and the last author [6,7] studied the dimension of the feasible region of densities ... the dimension and Lyndon permutations. We say that a permutation is Lyndon if the\ncovered by Theorem 1. However, the bound is not tight already for 3-point patterns: the\ndimension is ﬁve although there are only four non-trivial indecomposable permutations of\nsize at most three.\nBorga and the last author [6,7] studied the dimension of the feasible region of densities\nof patterns of size at most k and observed, utilizing a result of Vargas [48], a link between\nthe dimension and Lyndon permutations. We say that a permutation is Lyndon if the\nword formed by its indecomposable blocks is Lyndon (a formal deﬁnition is given in Sub-\nsection 2.4), and use PL\nk for the set of all non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most\nk. In particular, they noted that the dimension of the region of feasible pattern densities is\nat most the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations [6], and conjectured [6, Conjecture\n1.3] that this bound is tight. We combine methods from algebra and combinatorics to prove\nthis conjecture; note that the set PL\nk of non-trivial Lyndon permutations is a superset of\nPI\nk of non-trivial indecomposable permutations.\nTheorem 2. For every integer k ≥2, there exists x0 ∈[0, 1]PL\nk and ε > 0 such that for\nevery x ∈Bε(x0) ⊆[0, 1]PL\nk there exists a permuton Π such that\nd(σ, Π)σ∈PL\nk = x.\nTheorem 2 determines the dimension of the feasible region of densities of patterns of\nsize at most k in large permutations. Since the statement that there exists a polynomial\nfunction f : [0, 1]PL\nk →[0, 1]Pk such that, for every permuton Π, we have f(d(σ, Π)σ∈PL\nk ) =\nd(σ, W)σ∈Pk, is given in [6] without particular details, we give a direct proof of the existence\nof the function f in Section 4 (Theorem 8) for completeness. The presented proof (unlike\nthe one suggested in [6]) uses only basic properties of Lyndon words and ﬂag algebras,\nwhich are surveyed in Subsections 2.4 and 2.5.\nWe remark that properties of Lyndon\nwords seem to capture independence of “order-like” combinatorial structures and the ideas\npresented in this paper were followed in [29] to determine the dimension of the feasible\nregion of densities of subtournaments in large tournaments.\n2\nPreliminaries\nWe now introduce notation used throughout this manuscript. As we combine techniques\nfrom several diﬀerent areas, we introduce the relevant techniques from each of the areas in\none of the subsections that follow. Before doing so, we ﬁrst ﬁx some general notation. The\nset of the ﬁrst n positive integers is denoted by [n], and if A is a set of integers and k ∈N,\nthen A + k is the set {a + k, a ∈A}. A subset A of integers is an interval if A consists of\nconsecutive integers, i.e., there exist integers a, b ∈N, a ≤b, such that A = {a, a+1, . . . , b}.\nFinally, two subsets I and J of integers are non-crossing if max(I) < min(J) or vice versa.\n2.1\nGraph limits\nWe now introduce basic notations related to graph limits as developed in particular in [8–\n10, 35, 36]; we refer to the monograph by Lov´asz [34] for a comprehensive introduction\n3--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFeasible regions and permutation patterns (https://raulpenaguiao.github.io/docs/Slides/PP2021-Handout.pdf)\nciteturn2search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Lyndon permutations. ... The dimension of the feasible region is precisely the number of Lyndon\nFeasible regions on classical patterns  |  |  | \n--- | --- | --- | ---\nThe feasible region  |  |  | \nTheorem (Glebov et.al. 2014, Vargas 2014)\nThe dimension of the feasible region is bounded below by the number\nof indecomposible permutations, and bounded above by the number of\nLyndon permutations.\nConjecture\nThe dimension of the feasible region is precisely the number of Lyndon\npermutations.  |  |  | \nRau´l Penaguia˜o (San Francisco)  | Feasible Regions  |  | 15th June, 2021 3 / 12--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities |... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/the-dimension-of-the-feasible-region-of-pattern-densities-7894-1443563)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; This paper proves that the dimension of the feasible region for densities of permutation patterns of size at most k equals the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k, resolving the tightness of the previously established lower bound. ... This paper proves that the dimension of the feasible region for densities of permutation patterns of size at most k equals the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k, resolving the tightness of the previously established lower bound.\n\nBack ArXiv math.CO The dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities\n\nArXiv math.CO\n\n# The dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities\n\nAlexandru Malekshahian, Daniel Kral, Frederik Garbe, Raul Penaguiao\n\n2309.10203 Published Jan 24, 2024\n\nChat with paper Cite 中文 View PDF Share\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: Jan 24, 2024\n\nCategories: math.CO\n\n### AI Quick Summary\n\nThis paper proves that the dimension of the feasible region for densities of permutation patterns of size at most k equals the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k, resolving the tightness of the previously established lower bound. The proof utilizes an interplay between algebraic and combinatorial techniques related to Lyndon words.\n\n## Quick Answers\n\n### What is \"The dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities\" about?\n\nThis paper proves that the dimension of the feasible region for densities of permutation patterns of size at most k equals the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k, resolving the tightness of the previously established lower bound. The proof utilizes an interplay between algebraic and combinatorial techniques related to Lyndon words.\n\n### What methodology did the authors use?\n\nThe paper employs algebraic and combinatorial techniques, specifically focusing on Lyndon words and permutations, to analyze the dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities in permutations. More in Methodology →\n\n### What are the key results?\n\nThe dimension of the feasible region of densities of permutation patterns of size at most k is equal to the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k. — A lower bound for the dimension was previously established by Glebov et al., but the new result shows that this bound is tight for k ≥ 3. More in Key Results →\n\n### Why is this work significant?\n\nThis research contributes to the understanding of pattern densities in permutations, which has implications for extremal combinatorics and the study of large combinatorial structures. More in Significance →\n\n### What are the main limitations?\nWe prove that the dimension of the feasible region of densities of permutation patterns of size at most k is equal to the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k. The proof exploits an interplay between algebra and combinatorics inherent to the study of Lyndon words.\n\nShow full abstract\n\ndensities feasible permutation non trivial graphs trivial size vertices\n\n## Key Findings, in focus\n\nSeven facets of this paper, analysed and brought into focus by AI.\n\nAI-generated · verify against source Generated Sep 03, 2025\n\nIn focus · one-line synthesis\n\nThis research contributes to the understanding of pattern densities in permutations, which has implications for extremal combinatorics and the study of large combinatorial structures.\n\n01\n\nMethodology How they did it\n\nThe paper employs algebraic and combinatorial techniques, specifically focusing on Lyndon words and permutations, to analyze the dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities in permutations.\n\nCopy\n\n02\n\nResults What they found\n\n  * The dimension of the feasible region of densities of permutation patterns of size at most k is equal to the number of non-trivial Lyndon permutations of size at most k.\n  * A lower bound for the dimension was previously established by Glebov et al., but the new result shows that this bound is tight for k ≥ 3.\n  * The proof utilizes the interplay between algebra and combinatorics inherent to the study of Lyndon words.\n\nCopy\n\n03\n\nSignificance Why it matters\n\nThis research contributes to the understanding of pattern densities in permutations, which has implications for extremal combinatorics and the study of large combinatorial structures.\n\nCopy\n\n04\n\nTechnical Contributions What's new technically\n\nThe paper establishes a precise characterization of the dimension of the feasible region for permutation pattern densities, leveraging properties of Lyndon words and permutations.\n\nCopy\n\n05\n\nNovelty What sets it apart\n\nThe work extends previous results by Glebov et al. by proving tightness of the dimension bound for k ≥ 3, providing a more accurate description of the feasible region for pattern densities in permutations.\n\nCopy\n\n06\n\nLimitations What to be cautious about\n\n  * The findings are specific to permutation patterns and may not directly generalize to other combinatorial structures without further investigation.\n  * The methodology relies heavily on the properties of Lyndon words and permutations, which might limit its applicability to other areas of combinatorics.\n\nCopy\n\n07\n\nFuture Work Where it goes next\n\n  * Investigate whether similar results can be obtained for other classes of combinatorial patterns or structures.\n  * Explore potential applications of these findings in related fields such as statistical physics or network theory.\n\n### Impact\n\n55\n\nReferences\n\n### Paper Details\n\nPaper ID: 2309.10203\n\nLicense: http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\n\nCategories:\n\nmath.CO\n\nView on arXiv Download PDF\n\n### How to Cite This Paper\n\n[Button: BibTeX] [Button: APA] [Button: MLA] [Button: Chicago] [Button: IEEE]\n\n@article{malekshahian2024the, title = {The dimension of the feasible region of pattern densities}, author = {Malekshahian, Alexandru and Kral, Daniel and Garbe, Frederik and others}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2309.10203}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, primaryClass = {math.CO}, }\n\n### Authors\n\nAM Alexandru Malekshahian 3 papers DK Daniel Kral 4 papers FG Frederik Garbe 18 papers RP Raul Penaguiao 13 papers\n\n## Key Terms\n\ndensities (0.276)\n\nfeasible (0.272)\n\npermutation (0.261)\n\nnon trivial (0.260)\n\ngraphs (0.260)\n\ntrivial (0.239)\n\nsize (0.208)\n\nvertices (0.207)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Review of the article “Explicit asymptotics for signed binomial sums and applications to the Carnevale-Voll conjecture” by Laurent Habsieger (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/350153626_Review_of_the_article_Explicit_asymptotics_for_signed_binomial_sums_and_applications_to_the_Carnevale-Voll_conjecture_by_Laurent_Habsieger)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 months ago; # Review of the article “Explicit asymptotics for signed binomial sums and applications to the Carnevale-Voll conjecture” by Laurent Habsieger ... Enzo Bonacci reviews the article by Laurent Habsieger: “Explicit asymptotics for signed binomial sums and applications to the Carnevale-Voll conjecture” J. ... binomial sum;combinatorial identity;asymptotic analysis;orbit Dirichlet series;natural boundary\n\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# Review of the article “Explicit asymptotics for signed binomial sums and applications to the Carnevale-Voll conjecture” by Laurent Habsieger\n\n  * September 2020\n  * Zentralblatt MATH Zbl 1448.11049\n\nAuthors:\n\nEnzo Bonacci\n\n  * Institute of Physics\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nEnzo Bonacci reviews the article by Laurent Habsieger: “Explicit asymptotics for signed binomial sums and applications to the Carnevale-Voll conjecture” J. Integer Seq. 23, No. 6, Article 20.6.7, 32 p. (2020) Source: https://zbmath.org/?q=an:07212813\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nContent uploaded by Enzo Bonacci\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area was uploaded by Enzo Bonacci on Mar 18, 2021\n\n\nHabsieger, Laurent\n\nExplicit asymptotics for signed binomial sums and applications to the Carnevale-Voll con-\n\njecture. (English)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nZbl 1448.11049\n\nJ. Integer Seq. 23, No. 6, Article 20.6.7, 32 p. (2020).\n\nThe paper explores the following combinatorial conjecture: if λ 1 and λ 2 are two distinct integers (